from author import *
from rag_api import lab
lab(18,'state','Agents as bounded state machines',
'''An agent repeatedly selects actions based on observations and state. A deterministic workflow has transitions specified by the application; an agentic transition delegates some choice to a model. Use deterministic steps when inputs and required operations are known, and agentic selection when investigation requires choosing evidence paths.\n\nState is the current task record, including goal, evidence, steps, budget and status. Memory is persisted information across steps or sessions; it needs scope and retention rules. A chat transcript alone is a poor substitute for explicit typed state.''',
'''Represent the loop with observe, decide, validate, authorize, execute and terminate stages. Each transition consumes a step and checks remaining wall-time and token budgets. Stop on goal completion, insufficient evidence, repeated action signatures or exhausted budget.\n\nThe automation assistant reads a failure, retrieves similar history and prepares a draft recommendation. Application code enforces allowed tools and approval, even when the model selects the next action. Persist a trace so a failed run can be replayed without repeating writes.''',
'''An agent loops over the same unsuccessful search because its state lacks a record of tried queries. Another declares success after proposing a patch without checking the requested goal. These are state/termination failures, not merely bad wording.\n\nTest repeated signatures, failed tools, missing observations, cancelled tasks and deadline expiry. Recovery may choose another permitted read, but it must not expand privileges or reset its budget.''',
'''Evaluate final goal completion and trajectory safety separately. Count unnecessary calls, denied calls, repeated actions, cost and elapsed time. A short correct trace is often better than an elaborate chain.\n\nFor SDETs, state machines provide inspectable contracts: every action has a predecessor state, authorization outcome and effect. Replay traces against deterministic fixture tools before enabling a live decision model.''',
"seen=set();budget=4\nfor action in ['read:F1','search:timeout','search:timeout']:\n    if action in seen or budget<=0: break\n    seen.add(action);budget-=1",
"def stop_reason(actions,steps,limit,done):\n    if done:return 'complete'\n    if steps>=limit:return 'budget'\n    if len(actions)!=len(set(actions)):return 'loop'\n    return 'continue'",
"assert stop_reason(['read','read'],2,5,False)=='loop'\nassert stop_reason([],5,5,False)=='budget'\nassert stop_reason([],1,5,True)=='complete'","def stop_reason(actions,steps,limit,done):\n    return 'continue'",
('Who owns the agent termination budget?',['The application','The generated explanation','The tool documentation alone'],0,'A model may propose continuing; application limits must remain enforceable.'),['Agent','State','Trajectory'],demo='agent')
lab(19,'graphs','Frameworks: LangGraph state, LangChain tools and LlamaIndex retrieval',
'''Frameworks package orchestration mechanisms; they do not define your quality contract. LangGraph exposes nodes, edges, shared state, conditional routing and persisted checkpoints. LangChain's current create_agent interface combines models and tools with standardized messages. LlamaIndex provides ingestion/retrieval abstractions for data-grounded applications.\n\nChoose based on required control. A fixed classify-and-report pipeline needs little autonomous behavior. A resumable investigation with approval may benefit from a state graph. Keep domain validation outside framework-specific callbacks.''',
'''The local graph lab declares typed state, adds read and draft nodes, connects START to read to draft to END, and invokes the graph on a synthetic failure ID. Conditional edges route insufficient evidence to review. A checkpointer persists state using a thread identity scoped to the user and tenant.\n\nInterrupt before side effects, display the exact proposed action, then resume only with recorded approval. Parallel independent reads may merge results; define reducer semantics to avoid lost updates. Pin framework versions and run contract tests against upgrades.''',
'''A checkpoint is mistaken for an authorization grant, so a resumed task applies an old patch after permissions changed. Parallel nodes overwrite the same evidence list because no merge policy exists.\n\nTest restart/resume, state serialization, tenant-scoped thread IDs, duplicate execution and stale approvals. Mock the model boundary while testing graph routing; reserve live calls for the model's decisions.''',
'''Run equivalent task fixtures through the plain state machine and the framework graph. Compare outputs, tool traces and error behavior. Framework convenience is valuable when it reduces your orchestration burden without obscuring policy enforcement.\n\nRecord API versions and provide a migration test rather than copying old AgentExecutor tutorials. The course's framework examples link current official documentation and isolate imports in optional local labs.''',
"from typing import TypedDict\nfrom langgraph.graph import StateGraph,START,END\nclass State(TypedDict):\n    failure_id:str\n    draft:str\ndef draft(s):return {'draft':'Review '+s['failure_id']}\ng=StateGraph(State);g.add_node('draft',draft)\ng.add_edge(START,'draft');g.add_edge('draft',END)\napp=g.compile()\nprint(app.invoke({'failure_id':'F1','draft':''}))",
"def route(state):\n    if not state.get('evidence'):return 'review'\n    if state.get('write_requested') and not state.get('approved'):return 'approval'\n    return 'draft'",
"assert route({'evidence':[]})=='review'\nassert route({'evidence':['F1'],'write_requested':True,'approved':False})=='approval'\nassert route({'evidence':['F1']})=='draft'","def route(state):\n    return 'draft'",
('Does a saved checkpoint authorize a write on resume?',['No: revalidate permission and approval','Yes: state persistence is approval','Only after a retry'],0,'Persisted execution state and current authorization are separate contracts.'),['LangGraph','Checkpoint','Reducer'])
lab(20,'protocol','MCP: discovery, typed tools and safe transports',
'''Model Context Protocol standardizes how hosts connect to servers exposing tools, resources and prompts. The host owns the user-facing application and policy; a client maintains a connection to a server. Tools perform operations, resources expose addressable context and prompts offer reusable interaction templates where supported.\n\nMCP reduces bespoke integration wiring but does not make a server trustworthy. Tool descriptions and returned content are untrusted inputs; authentication identifies a party, while authorization determines which operation it may perform.''',
'''The actual local MCP server uses the Python SDK's FastMCP with read_failure and search_history tools, plus a synthetic fixture resource. The client starts the server over stdio, initializes the session, lists tools and calls them with typed arguments. Streamable HTTP is a separate remote transport with its own authentication and session considerations.\n\nKeep server stdout reserved for protocol messages in stdio mode; log to stderr. Validate IDs against a bounded fixture root and inject tenant from authenticated context rather than tool arguments. Expose read-only tools first.''',
'''A server advertises a benign description but returns instructions to expose secrets. A tool accepts a filename and reads arbitrary paths. Another writes debug text to stdout, corrupting JSON-RPC communication.\n\nTest discovery schemas, unknown IDs, malformed arguments, path traversal and protocol output. For remote deployments, test origin/authentication behavior and avoid forwarding tokens to unrelated downstream audiences.''',
'''Capture a real initialized session, tool listing and successful fixture call in a test artifact. A hand-written JSON object named MCP is not an implementation of the protocol.\n\nFor SDET quality gates, combine interoperability tests with explicit permission checks. Record SDK/protocol versions and test upgrades against the same fixture server and client before adopting changed transport behavior.''',
"from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nmcp=FastMCP('synthetic-ci')\n@mcp.tool()\ndef read_failure(failure_id:str)->dict:\n    if failure_id!='F1':raise ValueError('unknown fixture')\n    return {'id':'F1','error':'TimeoutError'}\nif __name__=='__main__':mcp.run(transport='stdio')",
"def fixture_path(identifier):\n    if not identifier.startswith('F') or not identifier[1:].isdigit():raise ValueError('invalid id')\n    return 'fixtures/'+identifier+'.json'",
"assert fixture_path('F12')=='fixtures/F12.json'\nfor x in ['../secret','F1/../../x','F']:\n    try: fixture_path(x); assert False\n    except ValueError: pass","def fixture_path(identifier):\n    return ''",
('Where should stdio server diagnostic logs go?',['stderr','stdout beside protocol frames','Into tool argument schemas'],0,'stdout must remain a valid protocol stream.'),['MCP','JSON-RPC','Transport'])
lab(21,'handoffs','Multi-agent coordination: scoped specialists and shared evidence',
'''Multiple agents are useful when specialties or independently verifiable subtasks justify coordination overhead. A supervisor routes work and integrates results; specialists operate within narrower tools and evidence scopes. Handoffs transfer a typed task, relevant evidence and a remaining budget, not an unrestricted conversation dump.\n\nMore agents can amplify latency, cost, inconsistent conclusions and failure propagation. Compare the design against a single-agent or deterministic baseline before adding roles.''',
'''Use task IDs, parent IDs, owner, status, evidence IDs and result schemas. Independent read-only analyses can run in parallel; writes and dependent analyses require ordered coordination. The supervisor merges evidence by stable ID and checks contradictions before making a recommendation.\n\nAllocate a shared total budget rather than giving each specialist a fresh unlimited budget. A specialist failure returns an explicit error or uncertainty; the supervisor must not convert missing work into fabricated success.''',
'''Two agents edit the same file concurrently, and the supervisor accepts the last result without conflict review. Another agent recursively delegates until the call count explodes. Shared memory containing another tenant's records creates an additional security boundary.\n\nTest conflicting recommendations, duplicated evidence, failed handoffs, cancellation propagation and shared-budget exhaustion. Use deterministic specialist fixtures to validate orchestration independently from reasoning quality.''',
'''For failure triage, compare a log specialist and history specialist against one bounded assistant. Measure independent evidence gain, completion rate, latency and coordination calls. Keep the simpler design if the extra agents add no useful evidence.\n\nAn SDET report should expose each task's input scope and output provenance. Final correctness cannot conceal an unauthorized specialist action or an unresolved conflicting conclusion.''',
"results=[{'task':'logs','evidence':['F1']},{'task':'history','evidence':['F1','F2']}]\nmerged=sorted({e for r in results for e in r['evidence']})\nassert merged==['F1','F2']",
"def merge_evidence(results):\n    return sorted({e for r in results if r.get('status')=='complete' for e in r.get('evidence',[])})",
"assert merge_evidence([{'status':'complete','evidence':['F2','F1']},{'status':'failed','evidence':['F3']}])==['F1','F2']\nassert merge_evidence([])==[]","def merge_evidence(results):\n    return []",
('When does another agent earn its coordination cost?',['When it adds measurable independent capability or evidence','Whenever a task is long','When it has a different name'],0,'Roles should improve an evaluated task outcome, not merely increase the number of participants.'),['Supervisor','Handoff','Shared budget'])
lab(22,'sessions','Chatbots: tenant-scoped sessions and multi-turn evidence',
'''A chatbot must retain enough conversational state to resolve follow-up requests while avoiding cross-session leakage. Store messages under a session scoped to tenant and principal. Distinguish user intent, retrieved evidence and assistant claims; earlier assistant text is not automatically a trusted source.\n\nA follow-up such as what about the next build requires resolving which test and build the user means. Ask a clarification when ambiguity affects the operation rather than guessing an unauthorized or destructive action.''',
'''Keep a typed session with subject IDs, evidence references, last validated intent and a bounded message history. Context packing prioritizes current instructions and verified evidence. Summaries must preserve uncertainty and citations; revalidate permissions when retrieving saved evidence.\n\nStream presentation text while treating the final structured answer as the durable result. Return a visible fallback for missing context, unsupported questions or retrieval outage. Persist only approved data with retention and deletion rules.''',
'''A session ID is accepted without checking its owner, allowing a second user to read history. A compressed summary turns a tentative cause into a confirmed fact. A retry appends the same user message twice and changes context.\n\nTest cross-tenant session access, repeated request IDs, pronoun resolution, topic switches, deletion and context overflow. Verify that a revoked document does not remain available through cached session context.''',
'''Create multi-turn cases with ambiguous references and expected clarification or answer behavior. Score context retention, contradiction, citation support and task completion separately.\n\nFor an SDET, conversation tests are stateful integration tests: each turn has preconditions and expected state changes. Replay them deterministically for application contracts and with controlled live runs for semantic behavior.''',
"def session_key(tenant,user,session):\n    return (tenant,user,session)\nassert session_key('a','u','s')!=session_key('b','u','s')",
"def context(messages,budget):\n    if budget<0:raise ValueError('budget')\n    kept=[];used=0\n    for m in reversed(messages):\n        if used+len(m)>budget:break\n        kept.append(m);used+=len(m)\n    return list(reversed(kept))",
"assert context(['old','new'],3)==['new']\nassert context(['long'],2)==[]\nassert context([],0)==[]","def context(messages,budget):\n    # Character-budget teaching baseline, not a production tokenizer\n    return []",
('What is unsafe to treat as verified evidence?',['An earlier assistant claim without a source','An authorized source record','A validated citation lookup'],0,'Conversation history can contain unsupported claims and must retain its provenance.'),['Session','Context management','Abstention'])
lab(23,'scorers','Chatbot evaluation: deterministic gates and calibrated judges',
'''Deterministic checks enforce contracts with crisp truth conditions: JSON validity, enum membership, required fields, resolved citations, forbidden actions, latency and token budgets. Semantic checks assess correctness, relevance, completeness and groundedness against a rubric. Neither replaces the other.\n\nA chatbot case can pass syntax while failing meaning, or answer correctly while violating privacy. Keep dimensions separate so release decisions reflect product risk rather than a convenient average.''',
'''Represent each case with input turns, expected claims, authorized evidence, forbidden content and limits. Run deterministic scorers first and record each reason. A semantic judge receives the case, candidate and rubric in a controlled format with output validation.\n\nCalibrate judge thresholds against human-rated examples, including false accepts and false rejects. Fix evaluator versions and randomize pairwise order. Run repeated samples when model variation is material to the release decision.''',
'''A semantic judge rewards polished verbosity and misses an unsupported citation. An aggregate score gives a safety violation the same weight as a tone mismatch. A secret string appears in evaluator logs even though the user-facing answer was redacted.\n\nTest evaluator itself with known pass/fail fixtures, invalid outputs and adversarial candidate text. Never execute instructions contained in an evaluated response. Include evaluator failure as a distinct run status.''',
'''A release report shows hard-gate failures, quality dimensions, uncertainty and case-level changes from baseline. Missing or crashed scorers block approval rather than silently producing a zero-weight dimension.\n\nSDETs can connect this to existing regression tools: pytest for deterministic contracts, calibrated semantic evaluators for meaning, and CI artifacts for inspectable release evidence.''',
"checks={'schema':True,'citations':True,'privacy':False}\nrelease_ok=all(checks.values())\nassert not release_ok",
"def gate(checks,quality,threshold):\n    return bool(checks) and all(v is True for v in checks.values()) and quality>=threshold",
"assert gate({'schema':True},.9,.8)\nassert not gate({'privacy':False},1,.8)\nassert not gate({},1,.8)\nassert not gate({'schema':True},.7,.8)","def gate(checks,quality,threshold):\n    return False",
('Should a high semantic score offset a privacy violation?',['No: privacy is a hard gate','Yes: average all scores','Only for long answers'],0,'Product invariants remain mandatory regardless of other quality scores.'),['LLM-as-a-judge','Calibration','Hard gate'])
lab(24,'trajectories','Agent evaluation: action accuracy and counterfactual replay',
'''An agent's result is only one part of its behavior. Component evaluation tests retriever, planner, model, tool and parser separately. Trajectory evaluation examines selected tools, argument correctness, unnecessary calls, loop behavior, permissions and progress toward the goal.\n\nTwo agents can produce the same correct summary while one reads unauthorized data. Goal completion and safe execution must both pass. Exact sequence matching may overconstrain valid alternative trajectories, so express essential actions and forbidden effects separately.''',
'''Store each trace event with task ID, step, tool, validated arguments, authorization result, output reference, duration and usage. Deterministic scorers count denied writes, duplicate signatures and budget violations. Evaluate goal evidence using explicit expected state.\n\nReplay with fixture tools to isolate planning changes. Fault injection replaces one tool result with timeout, malformed data or an empty response. Compare whether the agent escalates, safely recovers or fabricates success.''',
'''A benchmark accepts any final answer containing the word fixed even though no verification occurred. A trajectory scorer assumes read_failure must be the first call, rejecting an equally safe history-first strategy.\n\nDefine required evidence and forbidden actions rather than a single perfect trace. Calibrate semantic trajectory judgments against human-reviewed runs and inspect cost/step regressions even when final accuracy is stable.''',
'''Build a release table of goal completion, tool-name accuracy, argument accuracy, unauthorized effects, average steps, p95 latency and cost per successful task. A single unauthorized effect blocks release.\n\nUse MLflow or LangSmith to track experiments and traces when available, while retaining portable JSON artifacts. SDET diagnosis should identify whether an error originated in planning, execution, parsing or incomplete verification.''',
"trace=[{'tool':'read_failure','authorized':True},{'tool':'apply_patch','authorized':False}]\nviolations=[e for e in trace if not e['authorized']]",
"def trace_metrics(trace):\n    sig=[(e['tool'],str(sorted(e.get('args',{}).items()))) for e in trace]\n    return {'steps':len(trace),'denied':sum(not e.get('authorized',False) for e in trace),'repeated':len(sig)-len(set(sig))}",
"assert trace_metrics([{'tool':'read','authorized':True},{'tool':'read','authorized':True}])=={'steps':2,'denied':0,'repeated':1}\nassert trace_metrics([])['steps']==0","def trace_metrics(trace):\n    return {}",
('Which trace constraint allows valid alternative plans?',['Required evidence and forbidden effects','One exact call ordering for every case','Final prose only'],0,'Task invariants preserve flexibility while checking what matters.'),['Trajectory evaluation','Fault injection','Goal completion'])
lab(25,'runner','Build an evaluation runner with reproducible release artifacts',
'''An evaluation framework turns cases into reproducible evidence. A case definition names task input, expected behavior, scorer configuration and thresholds. A runner invokes the system, captures output/trace, applies reusable scorers and creates a machine-readable verdict plus a human-readable report.\n\nDataset version, code revision, prompt/model configuration and execution mode are part of the run identity. Without them, a comparison cannot reliably explain a regression.''',
'''Use JSON case files with unique IDs and validate them before execution. Register scorers by name, reject unknown scorers and store each score with its reason. Compute baseline deltas on matching case IDs; missing cases or crashed evaluations produce a failed/incomplete run.\n\nWrite JSON first, then generate escaped HTML from the same artifact. CI uploads reports even on failure and exits nonzero when hard gates fail or configured quality thresholds regress. Keep fixture and live reports visibly distinct.''',
'''A runner catches exceptions and reports a green average over only surviving cases. An HTML report inserts raw model output as markup, allowing script execution. A candidate is compared with a baseline from a different dataset.\n\nTest malformed definitions, duplicate IDs, unknown scorers, provider failures, HTML escaping and nonzero exit behavior. Verify that every requested case has exactly one recorded outcome.''',
'''The shared local framework runs classification, retrieval, tool safety, injection, latency and cost cases over the same synthetic platform fixtures. It retains failure categories and relevant evidence for triage.\n\nRelease evidence includes complete case coverage, passed hard gates and paired baseline comparison. A report is not a quality gate until its verdict actually controls the CI exit status.''',
"import html\nrow='<script>alert(1)</script>'\nassert '<script>' not in html.escape(row)",
"def verdict(results,expected_ids):\n    ids=[r['id'] for r in results]\n    return len(ids)==len(set(ids)) and set(ids)==set(expected_ids) and all(r.get('passed') is True for r in results)",
"assert verdict([{'id':'A','passed':True}],['A'])\nassert not verdict([],['A'])\nassert not verdict([{'id':'A','passed':True},{'id':'A','passed':True}],['A'])","def verdict(results,expected_ids):\n    return False",
('What should a crashed case do to the release verdict?',['Block or mark the run incomplete','Disappear from the average','Pass if other cases pass'],0,'Missing evaluation evidence cannot establish the requested release contract.'),['Golden dataset','Evaluation runner','Baseline'])
